/-!
# Order of jmath̄ at a zero divides 3

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.ord_jBar_dvd_three` (`aa3e7f78-314c-53bc-8afa-207c725b4220`), by
Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `1821b8e1-12e3-5fb3-ada8-796f439c5e95`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $N$ be a positive natural number. Write $F_N \subseteq \mathbb{Q}((q))$ for the intermediate
field [`ModularCurve.modularFunctionFieldFull N`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L305), the subfield of
the field of Laurent series over $\mathbb{Q}$ generated over $\mathbb{Q}$ by the family
`divisorExpansions N`, and let $\overline{F}_N =$ [`ModularCurve.modularFunctionFieldBar
N`](def/ModularCurve_ArithmeticGalois.html#L111) be the intermediate field of
$\overline{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ generated by the image of $F_N$ under the
coefficientwise ring homomorphism $\mathbb{Q}((q)) \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$ induced by
$\mathbb{Q} \hookrightarrow \overline{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`. Let $v$ be a place of
$\overline{F}_N$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ in the sense of the project's
[`AlgebraicCurve.Place`](def/AlgebraicCurve_DivisorClassGroup.html#L22), that is, a valuation
subring of $\overline{F}_N$ which contains the image of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, is not all of
$\overline{F}_N$, and is a principal ideal ring; for $f \in \overline{F}_N$ the integer
$v.\mathrm{ord}(f)$ is minus the logarithm of the value of $f$ under the adic valuation attached to
the height-one prime of that ring. Let $\bar\jmath =$ [`ModularCurve.jBar
N`](def/ModularCurve_MazurStepThreeInputs.html#L43) be the element of $\overline{F}_N$ given by the
coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion `jq` of the modular invariant. The assertion is: if
$v.\mathrm{ord}(\bar\jmath) > 0$, then $v.\mathrm{ord}(\bar\jmath)$ divides $3$.

This is the statement that the covering $X_0(N) \to X(1)$, realised here through the inclusion of
function fields over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, has ramification index dividing $3$ at every place
lying above the zero of the modular invariant, i.e. above the elliptic point $j = 0$ of order $3$;
no parity or other restriction on the level $N$ is imposed. It feeds the Riemann–Roch and genus
computations for $X_0(N)$, being cited in the construction of divisors attached to elements of the
Riemann–Roch space, in the integrality statements for regular differentials, and in the
identification of the genus of $\overline{F}_N$ with the classical genus formula.

